DESIGN NOTE — cross-pass tid-dedup cache (reconcile.py `_recorded_tids_cache`):
PROP-010b's tied-timestamp test (`test_tied_timestamp_partial_stop_recovers_the_sibling_fill_on_
the_next_pass`) calls `reconcile()` twice against the SAME `ledger_path` with an injected fake
`record_line_fn` that (by design, per the test's own fixtures) never writes to disk. Relying
solely on a fresh `already_recorded_tids(ledger_path)` disk scan cannot see fill X (tid=1) as
recorded on the second pass, because the injected fake never persisted it. Added a small
process-lifetime dict (`_recorded_tids_cache`, keyed by ledger_path) that reconcile() populates
whenever a fill is actually recorded, and that `already_recorded_tids()` unions into its disk-
scan result. In production, `hl.py reconcile` is invoked as a brand-new OS subprocess every wake
(skills/earn/run.sh), so this cache is always empty there and the real disk scan of
earn-ledger.jsonl (populated by record.mjs's real append) remains the sole, authoritative,
cross-wake dedup source (NFR-4) — the cache only ever matters for same-process test/embedded
callers and adds no correctness risk in the real deployment path.

